WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:06.200
Sometimes a movie performs so poorly and becomes
so poisoned by terrible word-of-mouth that

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:10.680
studios or distributors will pull it out of
theaters abruptly to end the embarrassment.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.740
Check out this round-up of box office disasters,
because you probably didn't see them in theaters.

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:21.320
Hollywood makes so many movies out of familiar
properties because name recognition equals

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:22.000
free marketing.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:26.740
It's much easier to get filmgoers on board
with something they already know than to sell

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:28.060
them on a new concept.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:28.980
That's how G.I.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:31.900
Joe and Transformers became successful movie
franchises.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:36.760
Ironically, though, another fondly remembered
cartoon show and toy line from the '80s became

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.140
one of the biggest film flops of all time.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:44.899
Jem and the Holograms, a live-action, modernized
version of the property about an all-female

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:49.580
rock band, opened in October 2015 in more
than 2,400 theaters.

00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:54.200
But the filmmakers didn't stay true to the
source material, thus alienating the only

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:56.340
people who'd actually want to go see it.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:02.240
The result was an abysmal rating on Rotten
Tomatoes and an opening weekend of just $1.37

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:03.060
million.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:07.440
In week two, revenues dropped to a mere $388,000.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:11.350
And that's when Universal pulled it out of
theaters completely and pretended it never

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:12.200
even existed.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:18.320
"We were just talking about you."

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:19.480
"Yeah, we know.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:20.480
We heard everything."

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:26.080
Inspired by the interactive audiences of The
Rocky Horror Picture Show, screenwriter Kenn

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:30.619
Viselman conceived of a film where kids were
encouraged to yell out their thoughts to the

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:31.640
characters on screen.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:37.280
The result was The Oogieloves in the BIG Balloon
Adventure, which was mostly a generic kids

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.830
movie with some brightly colored characters
named Goobie, Zoozie, and Toofie.

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:46.800
Plus, there were special appearances from
stars kids love, like Cloris Leachman and

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:47.500
Toni Braxton.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:52.300
Parents had little interest in paying for
their kids to a yell at a screen for 90 minutes

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:54.080
when they could do that at home for free.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:02.660
During its first weekend, The Oogieloves earned
just $448,131 at over 2,000 theaters.

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:07.439
Accounting for multiple showings at each location,
that works out to an average of about two

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:08.520
people per screening.

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:13.580
It somehow did even worse in its next two
weekends before the marketing visionaries

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.640
at Kenn Viselman Presents mercifully removed
it.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:18.900
"Ooh ooh ooh ooh Oogielove!

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:21.800
Oooogielove!"

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:25.600
"What fun little Oogieloves you are!"

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:32.840
In June 2015, a movie with the generic and
bland title of United Passions hit theaters.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:39.240
It's a mawkish story of the rise and greatness
of FIFA, the universally reviled worldwide

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:40.700
governing body of soccer.

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:46.880
It's so extremely pro-FIFA that it's no surprise
the organization provided most of the film's

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:48.200
$22 million budget.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:53.620
It's a bad movie on its own merits, but it
also fell victim to extremely bad timing.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:59.129
Its release came just days after the arrests
and indictments of more than a dozen FIFA

00:02:59.129 --> 00:03:04.240
executives on corruption charges and the resignation
of FIFA president Sepp Blatter.

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:09.180
The director publicly regretted having made
the film and even called it "propaganda."

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:14.740
United Passions played to 10 theaters in the
United States, where it earned a grand total

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:16.440
of $918.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.980
It then dispassionately dis-unified with theaters
after just one week.

00:03:22.089 --> 00:03:27.239
The Nightmare Before Christmas isn't the first
film to combine scary Halloween scares with

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.680
the trappings of the Most Wonderful Time of
the Year.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:35.560
In November 1984, Tristar Productions released
Silent Night, Deadly Night, a slasher movie

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:37.060
in the traditional '80s style.

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:41.660
But the villain wasn't a guy in a hockey mask
or a knife-outfitted glove.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:47.090
Instead, he was a crazed man named Billy who
killed people with an axe while dressed as

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:47.860
Santa Claus.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:54.329
More than 200 picketers assembled at two theaters
in Brooklyn, bearing signs that read "Santa's

00:03:54.329 --> 00:03:58.460
Not a Hitman" and "Deck the Halls with Holly,
Not Bodies."

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:04.780
That outcry prompted Tristar to cancel a big
TV advertising push, and some theaters refused

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:05.880
to book the movie at all.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:10.909
What would've been a much bigger release wound
up a moderate one, as Silent Night, Deadly

00:04:10.909 --> 00:04:14.280
Night ultimately played in about 400 theaters
nationwide.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:19.370
The outrage killed the film's prospects like
Santa killed his prey, and it was pulled from

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:20.880
theaters after two weeks.

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:22.320
"Bingo!"

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:35.780
Released in August 1981, Honky Tonk Freeway
is a pleasant enough ensemble comedy that

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:41.140
seems tailor-made to run on a low-wattage
local TV station on a Sunday afternoon.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:46.300
It's about a small Florida tourist trap of
a town called Ticlaw that's home to a small

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:48.400
zoo and a water-skiing elephant.

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:53.599
The mayor bribes a state official to ensure
that a new freeway will include an off-ramp

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:57.220
to Ticlaw, thus vastly improving the town's
fortunes.

00:04:57.470 --> 00:05:02.120
The off-ramp doesn't get built, so the residents
come together to paint the whole town pink

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:03.340
as a publicity stunt.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:08.660
All of that non-action is intercut with the
stories of various individuals about to wind

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:13.880
up in Ticlaw, including bank robbers, hitchhikers,
a family, and some nuns.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:21.820
Directed by Academy Award-winner John Schlesinger,
this whole affair somehow cost $24 million,

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:24.600
six million more The Empire Strikes Back.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:29.900
The total box office haul of Honky Tonk Freeway
was just over $2 million.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:33.420
It was gone from theaters without a trace
within a few weeks.

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:40.620
In the 2000s, Uwe Boll carved a niche in the
film industry as the go-to guy to turn video

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:41.500
games into movies.

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:46.520
Even when he lucked into working with great
actors, the results remained unimpressive.

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:51.409
Efforts like BloodRayne and Alone in the Dark
contributed heavily to the universally-acknowledged

00:05:51.409 --> 00:05:54.360
notion that video game movies are always terrible.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:59.120
But the one that rises above, or sinks below,
the rest is Postal.

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:05.120
This 2007 flick took the kill-'em-all action
of the Postal games and added a plot in which

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:09.400
the guy who played Farkus in A Christmas Story
is the title Postal Dude.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:15.900
The film includes dozens of gruesome deaths,
Boll playing himself getting shot in the groin,

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:20.980
and a supposedly comic scene lampooning the
events of 9/11, not shown here.

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:27.120
While that all seems like the plot of a direct-to-video
movie, or possibly a fever induced nightmare,

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:31.940
Postal somehow got itself a wide theatrical
release, scheduled for May 2008.

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:36.400
But just before its release, nearly all of
the distributors that had lined up pulled

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:36.900
out.

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:41.740
Rather than open on 1,500 screens, it opened
on four.

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:47.700
That's right, Postal was so bad that it got
pulled out of theaters before it even arrived.

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:53.660
Had Gigli not starred two of the biggest celebrities
of the era, who were in a high-profile romance

00:06:53.669 --> 00:06:58.860
at the time, it would've been just a weird
and forgettable movie, not a legendary flop.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:03.749
Ben Affleck plays a stereotypical gangster
goon with an unpronounceable name assigned

00:07:03.749 --> 00:07:09.440
to kidnap the Baywatch-obsessed, developmentally-disabled
brother of a federal prosecutor.

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:14.580
Things go awry, so the bosses send in a more
capable operative, Ricki, played by Jennifer

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:15.080
Lopez.

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:20.620
Then the two fall in love, even though Ricki
is gay and Gigli is a raging misogynist.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:26.580
That plot, and the off-screen "Bennifer" antics,
led to an unofficial competition among critics

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:29.220
over who could say the meanest things in their
reviews.

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:31.120
The Wall Street Journal called it,

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:37.280
"The worst movie, all right, the worst allegedly
major movie, of our admittedly young century."

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:44.100
Somehow, Gigli cost $54 million to make, but
it unsurprisingly bombed, bringing in just

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:44.600
$6 million.

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:49.879
After three weeks in theaters, it moved to
its final resting place in the grocery store

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:51.420
DVD bargain bins.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:56.920
Madonna is certainly one of the greatest and
most influential pop stars of all time, but

00:07:56.920 --> 00:08:00.160
that goodwill has very little to do with her
forays into acting.

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:03.720
She's basically the Meryl Streep of the Razzie
Awards.

00:08:03.729 --> 00:08:08.880
The "Oscars of bad movies" have dishonored
Madonna a remarkable 16 times for stinkers

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:12.849
like Shanghai Surprise, Body of Evidence,
and Swept Away.

00:08:12.849 --> 00:08:17.669
That last one seemed like it might be a little
better than the usual Madonna movie, as it

00:08:17.669 --> 00:08:19.420
was written and directed by Guy Ritchie.

00:08:19.789 --> 00:08:24.520
He's the acclaimed filmmaker behind Lock,
Stock, and Two Smoking Barrels who also happened

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:26.160
to be Madonna's husband at the time.

00:08:26.889 --> 00:08:28.700
That didn't seem to do much good, however.

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:33.579
The desert island rom-com suffered a critical
drubbing and took home four Razzies.

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:35.140
Audiences didn't like it either.

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:40.860
It played in American theaters for three weekends
to a total of less than $600,000 before the

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:42.680
distributor swept it away.

00:08:43.340 --> 00:08:48.500
In the late 2000s, a science-fiction allegory
about respecting nature and the dangers of

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:50.300
colonialism hit theaters.

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:56.060
It utilized cutting-edge CGI and animation
technology to render lifelike but exotic humanoid

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:56.560
creatures.

00:08:57.160 --> 00:09:02.400
But we're not actually talking about Avatar,
which pulled in a record $2.7 billion at the

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:05.840
worldwide box office and played to packed
movie theaters for months on end.

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:11.120
Nope, this is the story of Delgo, a movie
at the other end of the box office spectrum.

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:17.630
With the protagonists fleeing their resource-ravaged
homeland for another, this 2008 animated feature

00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:22.560
bore a similar look and premise to Avatar,
but it had far from the same success.

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:28.740
Made by the small Fathom Studios, development
began in 1999, when CGI animation was still

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:30.130
something of a novelty.

00:09:30.130 --> 00:09:36.140
By the time of its release in 2008, Delgo's
style was old hat, and not particularly well-executed.

00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:41.800
Plus, its lead voice actors, Freddie Prinze,
Jr. and Jennifer Love Hewitt, had lost their

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:42.920
A-list status.

00:09:43.310 --> 00:09:50.800
While Avatar broke just about every box office
record, Delgo broke one, too: it earned $511,000

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:57.260
at 2,160 theaters during its first weekend,
bad enough to make it the worst wide release

00:09:57.260 --> 00:09:59.120
opening weekend ever at the time.

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:04.500
It doesn't rank among the worst second weekends
of all time, though, because Freestyle Releasing

00:10:04.509 --> 00:10:07.920
yanked Delgo out of theaters just seven days
after release.

